DOCKER WORKSHOP — COMMAND CHEAT SHEET
========================================

Workshop: Dockerize Your Portfolio
Goal: Build → Containerize → Run


BEFORE THE WORKSHOP
-------------------

Make sure Docker Desktop is installed and running.

Check Docker:

    docker --version

What it does:
    Shows your installed Docker version.

When to use:
    To check whether Docker is installed correctly.


Test Docker:

    docker run hello-world

What it does:
    Downloads and runs Docker's test image.

When to use:
    To verify that Docker is working correctly.


========================================
1. BASIC DOCKER COMMANDS
========================================

DOWNLOAD AN IMAGE
-----------------

Command:

    docker pull nginx

What it does:
    Downloads the Nginx image from Docker Hub.

When to use:
    When you want to download an image without
    creating a container yet.

Think of it as:
    "Download a ready-made package."


RUN A CONTAINER
---------------

Command:

    docker run nginx

What it does:
    Creates a new container from the Nginx image
    and starts it.

When to use:
    When you want to create and start a NEW
    container from an image.

Remember:

    docker run
    = CREATE a new container + START it


SEE RUNNING CONTAINERS
----------------------

Command:

    docker ps

What it does:
    Shows currently running containers.

When to use:
    To check which containers are currently running.


SEE ALL CONTAINERS
------------------

Command:

    docker ps -a

What it does:
    Shows all containers, including stopped/exited
    containers.

When to use:
    When you want to see both running and stopped
    containers.

Easy way to remember:

    docker ps
        → Running containers

    docker ps -a
        → All containers


========================================
2. CONTAINER LIFECYCLE
========================================

STOP A CONTAINER
----------------

Command:

    docker stop my-docker-container

What it does:
    Stops a running container.

When to use:
    When you want to temporarily stop your application.

Important:
    The container is NOT deleted.


START AN EXISTING CONTAINER
---------------------------

Command:

    docker start my-docker-container

What it does:
    Starts an existing stopped container.

When to use:
    When you previously stopped a container and
    want to run that same container again.

Remember:

    docker run
        → Create + start a NEW container

    docker start
        → Start an EXISTING container


REMOVE A CONTAINER
------------------

Command:

    docker rm my-docker-container

What it does:
    Deletes a stopped container.

When to use:
    When you no longer need a container.

Usually:

    docker stop my-docker-container
    docker rm my-docker-container


========================================
3. FIRST DOCKERFILE DEMO
========================================

Create a project folder:

    my-docker-app/

Inside it, create:

    my-docker-app/
    ├── index.html
    └── Dockerfile


----------------------------------------
INDEX.HTML
----------------------------------------

Create a file called:

    index.html

Put this inside:

    <!DOCTYPE html>
    <html>
    <head>
        <title>My First Docker App</title>
    </head>
    <body>

        <h1>My First Docker Application</h1>
        <p>This website is running inside Docker!</p>

    </body>
    </html>


----------------------------------------
DOCKERFILE
----------------------------------------

Create a file called:

    Dockerfile

Put this inside:

    FROM nginx:latest

    COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html


What does it mean?

    FROM nginx:latest

        → Start with the Nginx image.

    COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

        → Copy our HTML file into the location
          where Nginx serves web pages.


========================================
4. GO TO YOUR PROJECT FOLDER
========================================

Command:

    cd C:\Users\YourName\my-docker-app

What it does:
    Moves the terminal into your project folder.

When to use:
    Before running docker build.

Note:
    Replace "YourName" with your own Windows
    username/path.


========================================
5. BUILD THE DOCKER IMAGE
========================================

Command:

    docker build -t my-docker-app .

What it does:
    Builds a Docker image using the Dockerfile
    and files in the current folder.


BREAKDOWN:

    docker
        → Use Docker.

    build
        → Build an image.

    -t
        → Give the image a name/tag.

    my-docker-app
        → Image name.

    .
        → Use the current folder as the build context.


FLOW:

    Dockerfile + index.html
              ↓
        docker build
              ↓
          IMAGE


========================================
6. SEE YOUR IMAGES
========================================

Command:

    docker images

What it does:
    Lists Docker images stored on your computer.

When to use:
    After building an image, to confirm that
    the image was created.


========================================
7. RUN YOUR DOCKERIZED WEBSITE
========================================

Command:

    docker run -d -p 8081:80 --name my-docker-container my-docker-app

What it does:
    Creates and starts a container from the
    my-docker-app image.


BREAKDOWN:

    docker run
        → Create and start a new container.

    -d
        → Run the container in the background.

    -p 8081:80
        → Connect your computer's port 8081
          to the container's port 80.

    --name my-docker-container
        → Give the container an easy-to-remember name.

    my-docker-app
        → The image used to create the container.


PORT MAPPING:

    YOUR COMPUTER          CONTAINER

         8081       →          80


The browser connects to:

    localhost:8081

Docker forwards it to:

    Container port 80

Nginx receives the request and serves:

    index.html


========================================
8. OPEN THE WEBSITE
========================================

This is NOT a Docker command.

Open Chrome and visit:

    http://localhost:8081


You should see:

    My First Docker Application

    This website is running inside Docker!


This is the main "WOW" moment of the demo.


========================================
9. CHECK THE RUNNING CONTAINER
========================================

Command:

    docker ps

When to use:
    Immediately after docker run.

What to look for:

    my-docker-container

You should also see a port mapping similar to:

    0.0.0.0:8081->80/tcp


========================================
10. STOP AND RESTART THE WEBSITE
========================================

STOP:

    docker stop my-docker-container

What happens:
    The container stops and the website becomes
    unavailable.


START AGAIN:

    docker start my-docker-container

What happens:
    The existing container starts again.

Then visit:

    http://localhost:8081


========================================
11. MINI CHALLENGE
========================================

Modify your index.html.

For example:

    <h1>Hi, I'm Your Name!</h1>

    <p>
        CSE (AI) Student |
        Developer |
        Robotics Enthusiast
    </p>


After changing the HTML, rebuild the image:

    docker build -t my-docker-app .


Then create a fresh container:

    docker stop my-docker-container

    docker rm my-docker-container


Run the updated image:

    docker run -d -p 8081:80 --name my-docker-container my-docker-app


Open:

    http://localhost:8081


Now your updated website is running inside Docker.


